/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (A positive output gap may be more likely to be self-correcting as it is difficult to sustain an output beyond the maximum potential for long. The higher costs that will be generated are likely to reduce output. A government may consider taking action to reduce an output gap if it is a negative output gap which will not be self-correcting and if it is prepared to accept a higher inflation rate. Whether a government should seek to reduce an output gap depends on the type of the output gap, whether the output gap will be self correcting and whether the measures it may take may harm its macroeconomic objectives)
/E (A positive output gap may be more likely to be self-correcting as it is difficult to sustain an output beyond the maximum potential for long. The higher costs that will be generated are likely to reduce output. A government may consider taking action to reduce an output gap if it is a negative output gap which will not be self-correcting and if it is prepared to accept a higher inflation rate. Whether a government should seek to reduce an output gap depends on the type of the output gap, whether the output gap will be self correcting and whether the measures it may take may harm its macroeconomic objectives)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [15]
